{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n62 \n \n \nWeek 14: June 7-13 \nOn GMT 156, ACME Flame Design test conditions resulted in flames which continued to produce soot \nfollowing ignition, yielding new information for this study of soot processes. While soot is both an \nenvironmental and health concern, it can be useful for some applications. As two examples, soot is helpful \nfor radiant heat transfer and for illumination. The purpose of Flame Design is to determine the conditions \ncontrolling the presence or absence of soot in non-premixed flames (i.e., where the fuel and oxygen are \nseparated by the flame\u2019s surface). With that understanding, engineers will be able to \u201cdesign\u201d flames that \nare either sooty or not depending on their needs. \n \nCAPTION TO VIDEO, WHICH IS NOT INCLUDED IN THIS LOG \nIn this test point video, pure ethylene (C2H4), which is a sooty fuel due to its double bond, was \nburned in roughly 36% oxygen (with a balance of nitrogen). At ignition, the flame is very sooty. \nThe broadband thermal radiation of the soot particles causes the bright yellow color of the flame. \nThe fuel flow rate is then ramped up to more than twice the flow rate at ignition, where the flame \ngrows larger as a result. The test conditions are such that the flame temperature is high enough \nto continue to produce soot particles, which are mostly carbon and form when the ethylene \nwithin the flame is hot enough to react with itself in the absence of oxygen (which is outside of \nthe flame). But the flame later weakens as the oxygen transport drops and the radiative heat \nlosses become important. The flame appears to no longer produce soot near the flame\u2019s end, but \nthe glowing soot clusters are still apparent. With the long residence time resulting from the \nmicrogravity condition, the clusters become especially large. \n \n \n \nWeek 15: June 14-20 \nOn GMT 164, Nick Hague replaced the bottles on the CIR Manifold #2 and the CIR Check Gas Manifold to \ncontinue supporting ACME Flame Design test points. \n \nAlso, in support of Flame Design test points, Christina Koch replaced the ACME Igniter Tip and the ACME \nSpherical Burner on GMT 165. ACME returned to performing test points on GMT 171. \n \nWeek 16: June 21-27 \nOn GMT 171 and 175, ACME Flame Design test points were performed with the new spherical burner that \nwas installed on GMT 165. This particular spherical burner is markedly better at achieving a spherical", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 62}}